 \begin{abstract}
-Aligning a language model with multiple objectives requires an explicit compromise between improvements over a reference policy. We introduce Nash Bargaining Preference Optimization (\NBPO{}), which maximizes the product of objective-wise improvements in entropy-regularized preference-game values. Each objective uses a policy-dependent opponent. A constrained proximal formulation handles the zero-surplus reference boundary; its dual yields shared inverse-surplus weights. We solve the resulting finite-pool problems directly and fit the language model to their pairwise log-ratio targets. Under joint strict feasibility, the population optimum is Pareto efficient in game-value space, and exact proximal updates achieve an $O(1/T)$ last-iterate objective gap. Controlled experiments support adaptive-game representations as nontransitivity increases. On four-objective UltraFeedback, however, the current one-stage neural results do not establish an advantage for Nash aggregation or adaptive opponents. Uniform scalarized DPO has higher judged objective means across three seeds, alongside instruction-following and safety regressions in the reported capability panel. Closely aligned teacher objectives and imperfect target realization motivate a diagnostic separating independently evaluated target quality, neural projection and fresh-response quality. Run on $200$ held-out prompts against a common four-response bank, it places the exact bargaining target ahead of the empirical source distribution it is solved from, by $0.027$ and $0.051$ on instruction following and helpfulness with intervals excluding zero, while the Nash, fixed-reference and utilitarian targets differ from one another by at most $0.0018$; the fitted pools keep that advantage without reproducing the target, and the fresh-response stage separates the arms differently again on one development seed. The current natural-language results remain inconclusive for the proposed mechanism.
+Aligning a language model with multiple objectives requires an explicit compromise between improvements over a reference policy. We introduce Nash Bargaining Preference Optimization (\NBPO{}), which maximizes the product of objective-wise improvements in entropy-regularized preference-game values. Each objective uses a policy-dependent opponent. A constrained proximal formulation handles the zero-surplus reference boundary; its dual yields shared inverse-surplus weights. We solve the resulting finite-pool problems directly and fit the language model to their pairwise log-ratio targets. Under joint strict feasibility, the population optimum is Pareto efficient in game-value space, and exact proximal updates achieve an $O(1/T)$ last-iterate objective gap. Controlled experiments support adaptive-game representations as nontransitivity increases. On four-objective UltraFeedback, however, the current one-stage neural results do not establish an advantage for Nash aggregation or adaptive opponents. Uniform scalarized DPO has higher judged objective means across three seeds, alongside instruction-following and safety regressions in the reported capability panel. An independent development diagnostic finds target improvements in instruction following and helpfulness, but little separation between NBPO and its exact-target controls. Neural fitting does not reproduce target distributions, yet can improve independently judged pool quality, leaving the natural-language advantage of the proposed mechanism unresolved.
 \end{abstract}
 
