-\section{Related Work}
-\label{sec:related}
-\paragraph{General pairwise preferences.}
-NLHF and its scalable variants optimize directly against general pairwise preference models rather than fitting scalar rewards \citep{munos2024nash,wu2025sppo,zhang2025iterative,calandriello2024online,rosset2024direct,zhou2025egpo}. Blackwell-style methods extend this view to vector-valued feedback and target sets \citep{blackwell1956analog,bhatia2020multicriteria}. PROSPER introduces a maximum-entropy Blackwell winner with a scalable single-policy update and an inner worst-case choice over objective weights and opponents \citep{zhang2026prosper}. \NBPO{} instead keeps one regularized game value per objective and applies Nash bargaining across the improvements over a shared fallback.
-
-\paragraph{Multi-objective alignment.}
-
-\kekim_gpt{\textbf{R24 --- Separate the aggregation rule from the order of aggregation over prompts.} PROSPER's adversarial objective weights are prompt-dependent, whereas NBPO's inverse-surplus weights operate on objective values averaged over prompts \citep{zhang2026prosper}. Thus the comparison changes both the compromise criterion and the ability to compensate for poor performance on one prompt with improvement on another. Write the two objectives side by side and include a two-prompt example showing the distinction. Retain a faithful prompt-wise PROSPER baseline and the matched global-maxmin control over NBPO's game values. Without this separation, an empirical difference cannot be attributed specifically to Nash bargaining. Make clear that the individual-rationality guarantee is about population-average objective game values, not every prompt or every stakeholder.}
-\reviewresponse{\textbf{R24 --- Partially addressed.} The global-maxmin and prompt-wise PROSPER rows are distinguished, with the two-prompt example retained. Current policy results and seed counts are in Table~\ref{tab:uf-objectives}; the adaptation contract does not imply reproduction of every published training step. Per-prompt negative surplus is reported as a selected-policy violation, not an infeasibility certificate.}
-
-MODPO, Rewarded Soups, and Panacea expose trade-offs through scalarization, policy interpolation, or preference conditioning \citep{zhou2024beyond,rame2023rewarded,zhong2024panacea}. MaxMin-RLHF and RMOD optimize weakest-objective criteria \citep{chakraborty2024maxmin,son2026rmod}. MOPO uses pairwise preferences without point-wise rewards, selecting a primary objective and lower-bound constraints with a lagged reference in its practical algorithm \citep{agnihotri2026mopo}. RACO resolves conflicts between DPO-style objective gradients \citep{chen2026raco}. These approaches differ from bargaining over reference-relative robust policy values.
-
-\paragraph{Social choice and bargaining.}
-
-\kekim_gpt{\textbf{R25 --- Correct the substantive classification of MOPO and discuss RACO.} The preceding paragraph incorrectly includes MOPO among methods requiring scalar objective rewards or transitive preference models. MOPO explicitly uses pairwise preferences without a point-wise reward assumption; its June 2026 revision also refreshes a lagged reference and constraint schedule \citep{agnihotri2026mopo}. Distinguish its primary-objective/constraint selection and reference-based comparisons from NBPO's symmetric surplus bargaining and policy-dependent adversarial opponent. Also position RACO, a recent reward-free method for conflicting objectives using preference-loss updates \citep{chen2026raco}; reward-free training alone is not new. A matched RACO comparison is useful, but distinguish its DPO-style representation from unrestricted cyclic preference modeling. This correction is necessary for a credible novelty argument, not merely broader citation coverage.}
-\reviewresponse{\textbf{R25 --- Addressed.} Related Work now describes MOPO as using pairwise preferences without point-wise rewards, with a primary objective, lower-bound constraints, and a lagged reference. It also positions RACO through DPO-style objective gradients, so reward-free training alone is not presented as NBPO's novelty; a matched RACO experiment is not reported.}
-
-The Nash solution and its Pareto, uniqueness, and affine-invariance properties are classical \citep{nash1950}. Social-choice work argues that alignment should state its aggregation rule explicitly \citep{conitzer2024social}. Multi-party RLHF studies utilitarian, Nash, and leximin welfare over party-specific learned rewards and separately considers reward-free von Neumann solutions \citep{zhong2024nashwelfare}; Nash bargaining also selects immediate task-gradient improvements in multi-task learning \citep{navon2022nashmtl}. Our contribution is not a new bargaining axiom, but a formulation and practical optimizer for applying the classical Nash rule to policy-dependent objective-wise preference-game values.
-
-\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
-\label{sec:conclusion}
-
-\kekim_gpt{\textbf{R26 --- Compare against the current Nash-welfare literature at the level of the optimized object.} The \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.05006v2}{September 7, 2026 revision of Zhong et al.}, now titled \emph{Provable Pluralistic Alignment: Multi-Party RLHF under Offline Human Feedback}, includes disagreement-adjusted bargaining and separately treats general preferences through a von Neumann formulation. Update the bibliography/positioning rather than relying on the older title. Explain that its welfare analysis concerns learned party rewards, Nash-MTL bargains over immediate gradient improvements, and NBPO bargains over robust policy-value surpluses. Nash welfare, inverse-improvement weighting, and reference subtraction are therefore not individually sufficient novelty. Besides fixed-reference Nash, fit scalar rewards to the same comparisons and apply Nash aggregation as a controlled baseline; this isolates the incremental value of the game representation without confounding it with the welfare choice.}
-\reviewresponse{\textbf{R26 --- Partially addressed.} Related Work distinguishes Nash welfare over learned rewards, von Neumann solutions, gradient bargaining and robust policy-value surpluses. Controlled and UF BT-RM--Nash comparisons are present. These observations do not establish a representation advantage on natural feedback.}
-
-\NBPO{} combines reference-surplus bargaining with adaptive preference-game evaluation and a direct finite-pool proximal solver. Controlled experiments support the adaptive representation; the current one-stage natural-language results do not establish a Nash advantage. The proposed development diagnostic separates target quality, projection and fresh generation before further scaling. Joint improvement may be infeasible, and exact population guarantees do not certify a fitted neural policy, direct reference wins or per-prompt safety.
